CS4254 Fall 2026 - Project 1: Basic Network Programming

Group members: Nate Obcemea (nateo) Denis Melara (denismel)

Language: Go (standard library only: fmt, os, net, strconv, strings)

Build:  make            (produces ./simpleclient) Run:    ./simpleclient [-p
(optional) port] <hostname> <VT Username> e.g. ./simpleclient -p 27993
sprinter2.cs.vt.edu <VT PID>


High-Level Approach The client is one Go program:

1. main() parses the command line. It takes either "-p <port> <hostname>
<username>" or "<hostname> <username>", with a default port of 27993. 
2.createConnection() opens a TCP socket to host:port with net.Dial and sends
"cs4254fall2026 HELLO <username>\n". 
3. handleConnection() loops: read one message from the server, check it strictly, and respond.
 - STATUS "cs4254fall2026 STATUS a op b": compute a op b (integer math;
   division rounds down) and send "cs4254fall2026 <answer>\n".
 - BYE "cs4254fall2026 <flag> BYE": print the 64-byte flag as the only line
   on stdout, close the socket, and exit.
 - Anything else: print an error and exit with status 1.
Messages are read with conn.Read into a 256-byte buffer. A message that doesn't
end in '\n' within those 256 bytes is rejected as too long.


Challenges Faced
- Message length: the project description caps messages at 256 bytes, so we
  check that each read ends in '\n' inside the 256-byte buffer.
- Validation: our first version trimmed whitespace, and it sent a default
  answer when a message didn't match. The project description says to reject
  anything that doesn't match the format exactly, so we added checks for field
  count, prefix, message type, number format and range, operator, and flag
  length.
- Output rules: the project description allows exactly one line of output (the
  flag),so we had to remove our debug prints.
- Avoiding crashes: an early version indexed segments[] before checking the
  field count, so a short message caused it to crash but now the length is now
  checked before any indexing.
- Building on rlogin: we lowered the go.mod version and set GOTOOLCHAIN=local
  so the build uses the Go installed on rlogin and never tries to download a
  toolchain.


Properties / Features of the Design
- Defensive parsing: every received message is checked in full before it is
  used it ends the program with a clear error.
- Enforces the 256-byte message limit on received messages.
- Clean output: on success the only output is the flag. On failure the program
  prints an error and exits with a non-zero code.
- Safe arithmetic: operands are checked to be 1-1000, so dividing by zero is
  impossible and multiplication can't overflow.
- No dependencies beyond the Go standard library. A static binary
  (CGO_ENABLED=0) with no IDE files.
- Every function has a header comment. Old code is left in as comments where it
  was changed.


Testing Overview 
- Real server: ran the client against sprinter2.cs.vt.edu and
  sprinter3.cs.vt.edu from rlogin, with and without -p 27993. Each run printed
  exactly one line (checked with "| wc -l"), and the flag matched secret_flags.
- Command-line checks: no arguments, the wrong number of arguments, and a bad
  flag ("-x") each print the usage line and exit 1.
